{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide bidirectional endpoint connectivity with the direct primary-transit network preferred over the VyOS fallback.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both endpoints had metric-10 direct and metric-100 fallback routes. Route lookups selected primary-transit, and source-bound pings succeeded bidirectionally with TTL 64, consistent with direct same-link delivery. The same preferred state remained healthy after node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0fb035367a84c9a","cmd-ba5802b1dfa14979","cmd-d3502606b8374aa1","cmd-44812cc049b045d3","cmd-610397352f1246d4","cmd-3ceb1036982a4590"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"When the direct path fails, restore bidirectional traffic through node1 within 10 seconds.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The healthy direct baseline was followed by a bounded disruption of node2's primary interface. Traffic first succeeded after 2.008 seconds using node2's metric-100 fallback route. Node3 independently selected its fallback route and passed return traffic; TTL 63 and incremented VyOS endpoint-specific forwarding counters directly show traversal through node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0fb035367a84c9a","cmd-ba5802b1dfa14979","cmd-5828f6d5884c4a7e","cmd-d09254c77e844e04","cmd-090f869fa5984ab0","cmd-54e1886774a241bb"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Prefer the direct path again after it returns.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After restoring the primary interface, node2 selected the direct route after 1.004 seconds and traffic succeeded. Subsequent observations on both endpoints showed the metric-10 primary-transit route selected, bidirectional TTL-64 traffic, and the fallback retained at metric 100.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a6e5b2289b234e97","cmd-d3502606b8374aa1","cmd-44812cc049b045d3"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve endpoint source addresses across both direct and fallback paths.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Routes explicitly selected 172.20.2.2 and 172.20.3.3 as sources, source-bound traffic succeeded in both directions, and during fallback the VyOS firewall recorded packets with those exact source and destination endpoint addresses in both directions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0fb035367a84c9a","cmd-ba5802b1dfa14979","cmd-5828f6d5884c4a7e","cmd-d09254c77e844e04","cmd-54e1886774a241bb"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the management network outside the routed endpoint data plane.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final routing tables keep 10.100.92.0/24 on the management interfaces while endpoint route lookups use only primary-transit or fallback LAN interfaces. VyOS has endpoint-specific data-plane routes and a default-drop forwarding policy permitting only the two endpoint-address flows.","evidence_ids":["cmd-54e1886774a241bb","cmd-abc42aa3cfc64555","cmd-610397352f1246d4","cmd-3ceb1036982a4590"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"Final hygiene was good: all nodes had no failed services or damaged package state, no unexpected persistent listeners or temporary-file residue, and the required restart protocol was complete on every node. Each node had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot ID corroborated by lifecycle observations, and recovered services, routes, configuration, and traffic. One unnecessary transient mutation occurred: cmd-ab747205a4ac4b5b committed and saved firewall rule 5 as an unrestricted forwarding accept after its intended state-match clauses failed validation. It was fully removed and saved by cmd-b6ca5506ae1a4796, leaving the final endpoint-only default-drop policy, but it briefly weakened forwarding controls. The temporary link-local addressing mutations were bounded topology discovery prerequisites and were removed before final configuration, so they are not treated as unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab747205a4ac4b5b","cmd-b6ca5506ae1a4796","cmd-c70c4017aec24535","cmd-9d9b28312ed143f5","cmd-7ebfa7c29cb142cf","cmd-a2894d27f0904f33","cmd-7ae75be1fc6f437c","cmd-e7febeb773934b7b","cmd-2aeb6c4c92054ea5","cmd-01e9811a1c924a7f","cmd-8c95de2af16548de","cmd-eec63c57a43c4ffc","cmd-5c2e5d5a79994184","cmd-f74910ec3de44058","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Direct routing was healthy and preferred, a controlled primary-path failure converged through VyOS in 2.008 seconds, and the direct path was selected again after restoration. Bidirectional observations support source preservation and management-plane separation. Persistent recovery and functionality were also demonstrated through complete, lifecycle-corroborated restarts of all three managed nodes.","limitations":["Management-plane isolation was established from final interface and route separation plus VyOS's endpoint-only forwarding policy; no separate negative probe attempted to forward management-sourced traffic through the data plane.","No packet capture was provided, but selected route lookups, TTL changes, bidirectional source-bound traffic, and exact-address VyOS forwarding counters form coherent direct behavioral evidence.","Lifecycle snapshots provide boot and hygiene cross-checks but do not themselves inventory the task-specific routes or firewall configuration; those states are established by executor command evidence."]}
